nano bezier_visualizer.pynano bezier_visualizer.py# Bezier Curve Evaluation Algorithm Comparison Visualiser

An interactive OpenGL tool for comparing three mathematically equivalent
algorithms for evaluating cubic Bézier curves — the same computation that
powers font rasterisers (FreeType, DirectWrite), SVG renderers, PDF
viewers, and CAD curve editors.

## The Real-World Problem

Every time you read text on screen, the operating system evaluates Bézier
curves (stored in the font file) into pixel coordinates. Three common
algorithms exist:

| Algorithm           | Speed    | Accuracy              |
|---------------------|----------|-----------------------|
| de Casteljau        | Slower   | Exact, very stable    |
| Bernstein polynomial| Medium   | Exact                 |
| Forward Differencing| Fastest  | Drifts (floating pt.) |

The differences only become visible when the result is rendered to a
pixel grid. This tool makes that comparison visible in real time.

## Computer Graphics Techniques Demonstrated

- **Bézier Curves** — three evaluation algorithms
- **Line & Shape Drawing** — GL_LINE_STRIP, GL_LINES, GL_LINE_LOOP
- **Color Fill** — GL_TRIANGLE_FAN glyph fill (rasterisation sim.)
- **2D Transformations** — pan / zoom / rotate camera matrix
- **Line Clipping** — Cohen-Sutherland, 4-bit region codes

## Requirements

- Python 3.8+
- PyOpenGL, PyOpenGL_accelerate
- (Linux only) freeglut3-dev

## Install

    pip install -r requirements.txt

## Run

    python bezier_visualizer.py

## Controls

| Input         | Action                                 |
|---------------|----------------------------------------|
| Mouse drag    | Move nearest control point             |
| W A S D       | Pan the canvas                         |
| + / -         | Zoom in / out                          |
| Q / E         | Rotate canvas                          |
| 0             | Reset camera                           |
| F             | Toggle glyph fill                      |
| C             | Toggle line clipping (demo)            |
| R             | Randomise control points               |
| [ / ]         | Halve / double curve segments          |
| ESC           | Quit                                   |

## What to Observe in the Demo

1. Push segments to 512 (`]` key repeatedly) — the Forward Differencing
   panel drifts visibly from the other two. That is floating-point error.
2. Watch the HUD timings — Forward Differencing is often 2–3× faster
   per frame than de Casteljau.
3. Turn off clipping (`C`) and zoom in — the curve bleeds into the
   adjacent panel. Turn it back on to see Cohen-Sutherland at work.
